ram what i didn't have was a harvard  degree  near the top of the class  and a clerkship  at the supreme court level and the  individual whose class i taught was a  young teacher who i had met because he  was following around monitoring my work  for the justice department who had no  experiences of his own but had gone to  harvard had clerked at the supreme court  level and therefore had their credential  so he came back and said well  we don't have any openings for someone  with your expertise so i said okay but  two years later  after all of the events the  assassination the riots suddenly they  were very interested in me both harvard  and a number of the other major major  schools and i was i subsequently was  offered and accepted a position at  harvard but for me to go to that  position and think that it was simply my  talents  and not the circumstances  would be crazy  i saw  that i was an illustration of a pattern  as old as racial history  that the fact that i felt deserving of  an offer there was simply not enough  they had to sense that there was  something of value to them  something of their own interests before  they were willing to take a risk with me  i risked both in terms of being the  first black there and also as far as i  can see the first person who they had  hired who didn't have their credentials  and so my pioneering the barriers that i  had to overcome were twofold one is the  first black with all the presumptions of  black incompetence that in our that our  society  places on blackness and also all the  presumptions of non-worthiness  that many of the folk many of the  faculty there had because i was i didn't  come along the route that they that they  came what year did you begin teaching at  harvard 1969  and i was tenured in 1971.  it's it's only two years but older  people i was 38 at the time  are given a shorter period uh in which  to  for them  to make a decision whether to place you  on a permanent faculty  your book yes now uh before i want to  ask you about chapter seven as a way of  talking more about the harvard  experience a law professor's protest  but before we do that what technique  have you used in this book fiction and  non-fiction a fiction and sort of a  series of allegorical stories  um otherworldly uh fables if you will  and the reason i did that is that first  i like to tell stories and then i think  a lot of lawyers are are writers who are  scared to go through the apprenticeship  period the cold water flats the  unemployment and what have you that  writers have to  survive  um  but they still have that feeling that  they'd like to write  the other thing that is race is so  controversial that people all have such  they don't have to go to school they all  have deeply felt views based on what  they think is their experience  that in my views my perspective is so  jarring  that to simply state it is is very  upsetting and i find that i have a  better chance of at least being heard if  i can place those views in the context  of a fable of a story during the telling  of which or the reading of which the  individual is able to pull back to not  feel threatened or involved and at least  to kind of see this is something out  there  and people love stories as the success  of the soap opera john reproves  and i feel that they may not agree with  me at the end but at least they have  been able to considering this without  having to make an emotional commitment  uh to it when you read a fact in the  book is it accurate  for the most part i think i at least i  hope so well let me give you for  instance you tell the story about  meeting the white woman in the out in  the  countryside in oregon did you spend time  in oregon yes but the the actual meeting  heard is is not it's not factual it's  all fiction that part is fiction yes so  there's a mix of fact in fiction often  interwoven what you did in 1985 become  the dean of the law school at the  university in 1981  81 yes and i was deemed there for five  years  uh let me go back to this chapter seven  a law pressures professor's protest it  starts off by you saying that um  uh 196 black professors were meeting  with the president of harvard in his  home  and a bomb blew the place up kill them  kill them all right that's not a fact  it's not a fact but the the genesis of  that is that i was the co-chair  of the harvard black faculty and  administrators a group of blacks at uh  uh on on the campus at harvard  and we had decided to do a survey of the  effectiveness of affirmative action  across the campus and we interviewed all  of the deans got all their explanations  wrote them up sent them back and all and  then it was time to write the report and  i said now how can i write a report  that's not going to be read put on the  shelf put in the wastebasket and what  have you  and i decided to create a story  that would illustrate the kind of crisis  out of which  change progress if you will takes place  and i imagine in this story that all of  the faculty the black faculty were  meeting in the president with the  president on a beautiful saturday  afternoon in cambridge  and that suddenly there's this  horrendous explosion and the building  simply vaporizes and of course all of  those who are killed and then that there  of course would be a tremendous  investigation and an awful lot of what  was going to go on there would be  revealed from individuals files and what  they had told their wives or what have  you before they went off to this fatal  meeting  and you i was able through that  methodology to  to tell  all the things the information that  would generally go into a  straightforward report  one of the things i then imagine is that  if the president was going to as a  step in the right direction to issue a  really a positive affirmative action  plan if that kind of information worked  to come out in the wake of such a  tremendous tragedy there would be a  great movement to adopt it as a memorial  to the lost people  and i suggested it was adopted and then  indicate well of course there was not an  explosion but if there had been  this response is likely to have happened  and this response would be appropriate  even if it did if there were not such a  great tragedy and why didn't we do it  and then i sent out this report for the  discussion and what have you well the  president was not pleased  i went to meet with him and he said  why did you do this  and he was sort of sitting like this mr  president this was derek bach  and he said  you don't have to blow me up in order to  make your point you see and that was the  general reaction that they generally i  think they did not get the point  that they did not get the point that  they would have done  willingly  in the wake of a tragedy  as a means of compensation  uh something that would have been  appropriate to do even the absence of  that tragedy which was the reason i told  the story it's actually a  personal thing  you're  it bitterness comes out of the book you  even admit to bitterness in the book uh  it's sometimes the rhetoric is very  angry but when you sit and talk to you  here it's a yeah pleasant conversation  do you deal with people one-on-one like  this or do you deal do you ever get  angry visibly angry in front of them  i sometimes do  i find i'm not as effective  i'm hoping that while i want to be as  honest as i can it's very difficult to  be  in deemed a success  in a society where  thousands early millions of those who  look like you  are  in a situation a life situation than it  that was that is worse than perhaps any  time since  the reconstruction  and feel basically powerless  to do anything about it to see a  presidential campaign going on right now  in which there is for political reasons  rather virtually no mention of the the  kind of uh quasi-genocide  uh that is going on in many of the inner  cities of our of of of our nation  um and so that  uh i'm accosted of being a  you know despairing and to be uh  discouraging young people uh from uh  picking up the picking up the gauntlet  and continuing the struggle  and i guess part of my response is that  i want to discourage  them from  continuing the battle that i fought when  i thought i was fighting as a as a young  as a young lawyer this idea that racism  is  merely an aberration when it's really a  key component because i think we we  fought with the wrong with the wrong  tools maybe we had to do what we did in  order to recognize what i think more of  us recognize now but the other answer i  give is that  uh if if you are not discouraged  uh in in in sense  despair when you look at the condition  of the black community where is maya  angelou who i quote there  indicates that uh  uh young black males particularly see  others like themselves as the worst  enemy  uh that they confront with guns and and  what have you uh then uh i don't think  if you're not discouraged by that and  you're discouraged by my book then then  you need to perhaps reevaluate why  you're why you're despairing back to the  harvard situation you're now visiting  professor at new york university that's  right  how did you leave harvard well i decided  to protest  um the failure to of this faculty to  hire and tenure a woman of color i  throughout my time there i recognized  that the pressures of the students  were a large factor in my being hired  and i've always felt an obligation  to them and to myself as a pioneer to  try to open up the the hiring process  certainly for other people of color but  for whites as well who have  non-traditional qualifications as i did  and i've had some successes and and some  setbacks  i considered it a serious setback that  they were unwilling  to offer a permanent position to a black  woman who i thought had come  and and carried through many of the uh  carried out many of our hopes of what a  a woman of color would be able to do on  that faculty and yet because she didn't  have the credentials  that they wanted because although they  were much better than my credentials  because her writing was not traditional  in a way that they felt writing should  be  they rejected her out of hand  and it was very frustrating for me to  sit there and have  less good credentials to say this is a  person i think  i want you to look at what i've done and  listen to me and give weight to what i  am suggesting and have my suggestions my  recommendations uh simply  dismiss uh to have  black students urging and petitioning  particularly black women urging the  petition that they needed a person like  this and have those petitions rejected  out of hand was very  very upsetting so i decided i wrote a  letter to the dean sent copies to all  the faculty first on a private basis  and said  i can't any longer  pretend that i can be a model for these  women i can't pretend that i can be what  they want  and i'm going to withdraw from the  faculty until you give them what they  have been urging  well i took one year's leave and nothing  happened  i took another year's leave and still  nothing happened  and  the university has a rule limiting  leaves to two consecutive years which i  think is a good rule to make those  who've come down to washington and take  fancy jobs to make a decision whether  they want the faculty position or not i  argue that it should not apply  to my protest which is was a very  different character was in keeping with  what i told them i was going to do when  i when i was hired to continue  pressuring for more diversity in the  hiring process  they didn't agree so  as of june 30 of this year they  terminated my  terminated my position  geneva crenshaw yes who is she geneva is  a  consolidation or a  fictional product of many black women i  have known  my mother  my late wife jewel bell who died two  years ago after 30 years of marriage  constance baker motley the now federal  judge who i assisted in some of the  early cases in in 19 in the  in the early 