  of like our rico laws they'd lose the  business and what have you  um  it's not that i really i i argue with  geneva who suggests this plan to me  and i'm not i don't think the idea that  we should seriously  propose such a leg a piece of  legislation but that when we think about  it it is a it is a vehicle for  review of what our present laws do the  fact is that such a law might result in  less discrimination than we now have  under existing laws for a couple of  reasons one is that we see that people  who discriminate like mad  don't want to consider themselves racist  and if they had to post this sign  that would certainly be  the natural  normal conclusion moreover if they had  to pay for their racist  practices that takes a lot of the fun  out of it you see  the reason that they serve me is not  because they either love or hate me  because they see the profit in serving  me they don't see the profit and hiring  blacks who might just might be  unwelcome to some of their other  customers who might be unwelcome to some  of their other employees and so on so  why why do it uh but this provides a  reason to do it so that you might find  at least some  uh  lessening of discriminatory practices  simply by the pressure  with regard that this law would would  provide do you think about or do you see  discrimination or racism every day of  your life  no  no  but i'm i've been insulated i might be  stopped by the police but i know that  because i'm black but i know that when  they before i even reach in my pocket  carefully  uh  that the the  my standard english skills  automatically tell them that they hey  don't don't mess with him you see  i've been stopped in the south  by policemen who are ready to give me  the treatment at least verbally and once  i i always wore my uniform when i was in  the service but even since  once i pull out my wallet and there's  the harvard  officer's card there and what have you  they see that this is and i get almost  deferential uh treatment so that  education and status  economics stuff  poor whites always respect  upper class people you see they may not  like them  but they whether they're black or white  they get a measure of respect  quick story  when i was in louisiana  i wasn't there to challenge you but it  would seem that from what i've been  telling you i decided once beautiful  fall saturday afternoon that i'd love to  go to a football game  and in this little town there was a  small college and they were having a big  game  so going to a football game now i put on  my uniform shined up my bars  went the crowd was going in i parked my  car  and i went over to a policeman  big huge guy and i said officer  are blacks being permitted  to go to this game  he said lieutenant  i don't know but let me find out in this  big huge hawking guy i could see him  above the crowd swirling toward the uh  toured the stadium as he went first to  this window and then somebody sent him  around there kind of almost at a jog  and he finally came back to me he was  kind of walking slow  and he looked  embarrassed  he said  sorry lieutenant  they're not letting you in  they're not letting you in  i thanked him went back to my car and  and went home  but it see that's  that's the kind of thing i get i don't  get the direct uh beating on the heads  that so many of my less  less successful brothers  have to have to deal with  that it's not that we don't face  discrimination but it's a much more of a  mass thing i can go to a dinner party  with upper class professional blocks  and you would think that we were all in  prison  there is rage going around the dinner  table with the beautiful linen and the  handsome  uh cutlery and and silver and what have  you as they talk about one experience  after another that they that they have  gone through in their upper class in  their upper class status you see how  often  do people experience that other blacks  that you know a lot a lot what are the  where do they see the glass ceiling  phenomenon  of the  helping to train others who then get  promoted  over them uh the condescension uh the  unknowing uh slights people don't even  recognize what they are what they are  saying  uh sometimes they're not intended as  that sometimes it's our super  sensitivity  paranoia really  but not all paranoia is without  justification  but it means you really don't feel a  part of it  you see it harvard i think part of my  frustration  was that i played by the rules  i was determined that  notwithstanding that i was the first  black notwithstanding the fact that i  didn't have the traditional credentials  that i was going to become a better  teacher than most of them and i think i  did that i was going to become a more  noted scholar than most of them and i  think i did  and yet i  never became part of the family i was  never really accepted as as as one of  them so while that i had the status  and the pay and the opportunity to teach  some very very impressive students of  all races  i was never part of the family and  that's a that can be a very destructive  thing over time do you have any heroes  yeah  i think other people who stand up  and take risk  judge hasty who i may have mentioned  earlier who during world war ii left a  very plush position with the uh  with the department of army because he  was frustrated about the lack of  effort to end discrimination in the  in the service  next week on book notes our guest is  retired general norman schwarzkopf he  joins us to discuss his autobiography it  doesn't take a hero  that's next sunday on book notes at 8 00  and 11 pm eastern time  coming next a live news conference from  little rock arkansas with members of the  congressional democratic leadership who  met with president-elect bill clinton  last night  [Music]  this program is part of c-span's  a day


